The beer and diapers story is the famous one — supermarket analysis supposedly found that men buying diapers on Friday evenings also tended to buy beer, so the store moved them closer together. True or not, it captures the idea: you're looking for combinations that show up together more often than you'd expect by chance.

Lift is the key number. If 60% of all customers buy milk anyway, finding that 62% of customers who bought cereal also bought milk isn't interesting — the lift is barely above 1. But if 80% of customers who bought espresso also bought a specific flavored syrup, and that syrup is normally in only 10% of baskets, that lift of 8 is a real pattern worth acting on.

